**Source:** https://newsletter.rb2b.com/p/i-spent-350-000-on-youtube-and-failed-here-s-why-i-m-doubling-down  
**Author:** Adam Robinson  
**Date:** October 3, 2025

---

## Context

- Spent $350,000+ and hundreds of hours on YouTube
- Only 3,000 subscribers, <200 views/video
- LinkedIn founder brand drives ~80% of RB2B's 30K monthly unique visits
- Believes YouTube can become next major channel (benchmark: Instantly.ai — 64K subs, 3K avg views)

## What failed

| Format | Why it failed |
|--------|---------------|
| 18-episode Billion Dollar Challenge docuseries | Incredible storytelling but "not a YouTube video" — zero distribution |
| UnF*ck My Startup LIVE weekly show | Wrong format, bad hooks/thumbnails, not native YouTube even if improved |
| LinkedIn talking heads as Shorts | Never worked on YouTube; stopped working on LinkedIn |

## New strategy (with Samu Kovács / KS Media)

**1x weekly YouTube-native video:**

- 70% General SaaS Marketing Strategy
  - How AI ran my whole $6M ARR startup for a week
  - BEST SaaS Marketing Strategies for 2025/2026
  - How to use LinkedIn to grow your B2B Business

- 30% Direct Product Videos
  - How to Identify & Contact Your Website Visitors
  - Turn Website Visitors into Leads in HubSpot CRM
  - Get LinkedIn Profiles of Your Website Visitors

## Success metric (12 months)

Channel looks like Instantly's: 64K subs, >3K avg views/video, all content niche for buyer persona (early-stage SaaS/marketing).

## Key takeaway

"Just because you tried a channel once doesn't mean you shouldn't try again." — Same pattern as email (turned off, now crushing).

# Devin Reed — 95-5 Rule (HubSpot)

**Source:** https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/95-5-rule-gong  
**Date:** June 2, 2025

---

## Background

Devin Reed, former Head of Content at Gong (sales background). Built Gong's brand from $20M to $200M ARR with top-of-funnel content strategy.

## The rule

95% of your market isn't actively buying at any given time. 5% is in-market. Most marketing fights over the 5%.

## Gong's hard rule (Year 1)

**No product talk.** Zero features, comparisons, or demos from content team. Product marketing handled separately.

Mission: "Produce the most engaging sales content on the planet" — peer, not vendor.

## Multi-format distribution

Same value, different formats:
- LinkedIn posts + blog for readers
- Podcasts for audio-first
- Webinars + **short videos** for visual learners

## Results at Gong

| Metric | Before → After |
|--------|----------------|
| LinkedIn followers | 12K → 220K+ |
| Podcast downloads | 100K in 18 months |
| Webinar registrations | 500 → 2,500 |
| Email open rates | 28% (vs 15% industry) |

## Brand metrics (not last-touch)

- Audience growth across channels
- Direct web traffic (brand recall signal)
- Inbound opportunities without outbound chase
- Sales reps using content in calls

## YouTube strategy implication

Video content serves the 95% — educational, problem-focused, zero product pitch. When they're in-market (the 5%), they already trust you. YouTube is the long-form layer in this flywheel; pair with LinkedIn clips and blog transcripts for full coverage.

Devin Reed now runs The Reeder consultancy applying this system to B2B SaaS companies.


# Nate Woodbury — LEAF Strategy Summary

**Source:** Be The Hero Studios blog (July 2025)  
**URLs:** 
- https://blog.betheherostudios.com/the-leaf-strategy-how-to-rank-1-on-youtube-even-without-going-viral/
- https://blog.betheherostudios.com/dominate-youtube-search-step-by-step/

---

## LEAF = Long-tail Educational Answer Framework

Tree analogy:
- **Trunk** — Core expertise (e.g., B2B marketing)
- **Branches** — Subtopics (e.g., ABM, cold email, demand gen)
- **Leaves** — Specific 8-12 word questions with monthly search volume

## Step-by-step

1. Pick one branch aligned with expertise + highest buyer potential
2. Use Keyword Magic Tool with questions filter + 8+ word count
3. Film leaf videos: 10-12 min, educational, conversational (Zoom OK)
4. Separate marketing videos (search) from nurture videos (subscribers)
5. Batch film 20 episodes; release 1/week (5 months) or 5/week (1 month)

## Why it works for B2B

- B2B buyers search intentionally on YouTube ("how to set up ABM for SaaS")
- Long-tail questions have low competition
- Small consistent wins compound → eventually rank high-volume terms
- Works for Google AND ChatGPT citation (2025+)

## CTA framework

Single CTA per video:
- "Book a call" OR
- "Get this free guide"

## Practitioner scale

Nate produces 60+ channels using this exact system. $10K from 100 views cited on podcast — revenue per view matters more than total views.

## B2B SaaS application

Map your product's problem space as branches. Each leaf = one searchable pain point your ICP googles/YouTubes before buying. Dominate branch before expanding.

# Rehan Kadri — Content Repurposing Engine

**Source:** https://www.therehankadri.com/blog/content-repurposing-engine-for-youtube-linkedin-and-seo  
**Date:** March 25, 2026

---

## Core thesis

Most repurposing fails because teams force one asset into formats it wasn't built for. Better approach: choose core idea first, define channel transformations, create once with distribution in mind.

## Start with depth

Source asset needs texture: long-form video, workshop, internal memo, founder interview. Shallow source = repetitive downstream assets.

## Transformation rules

| Channel | Format |
|---------|--------|
| YouTube | Full reasoning, examples, pacing |
| LinkedIn | One argument + one proof + one takeaway |
| SEO | Decision-stage structure, search intent, subtopics |

## Editorial assembly line

1. One recording → transcript + talking points + examples
2. Pull clips, hooks, quotes from same transcript
3. Store approved phrasing in content library
4. Publish on cadence where each asset reinforces the next

## Measure resonance

Signals: watch time, saves, echo comments, branded search lift, direct replies, inbound conversations shaped by content.

**Not:** raw publish volume.

## B2B application for YouTube playbook

YouTube is the **source file** in a B2B content engine. One 15-20 min video becomes:
- LinkedIn clips + long-form post
- SEO blog post (decision-stage)
- Sales enablement timestamps
- AI/LLM indexable transcript (.SRT upload recommended)

